      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "To evaluate whether patients with silent myocardial ischemia during exercise testing are at increased risk for developing a subsequent acute myocardial infarction or sudden death, the data on 424 such patients with proven coronary artery disease (CAD) from the Coronary Artery Surgery Study (CASS) registry were analyzed. These patients (group 1) were compared with 456 other patients with CAD (group 2) who had both ischemic ST depression and angina pectoris during exercise testing and with 1,019 control patients without CAD. The probability of remaining free of a subsequent acute myocardial infarction or sudden death at 7 years was 80 and 91%, respectively, for group 1, 82 and 93%, respectively, for group 2 (difference not significant, compared with group 1), and 98 and 99%, respectively, for the control patients (p less than 0.001), compared with group 1 or 2). Among patients in group 1, the probability of remaining free of myocardial infarction and sudden death at 7 years was related to the severity of CAD and presence of left ventricular (LV) dysfunction, and ranged from 90% for patients with 1-vessel CAD and preserved LV function to 38% for patients with 3-vessel CAD and abnormal LV function (p less than 0.001). Thus, patients with either silent or symptomatic ischemia during exercise testing have a similar risk of developing an acute myocardial infarction or sudden death--except in the 3-vessel CAD subgroup, where the risk is greater in silent ischemia. The risk of patients with silent myocardial ischemia is based primarily on angiographic variables."},
